  "query_preview": "..\nTonight - Monday: A cold core upper-level low will begin its \ndrift southeast losing grasp of the Inland NW by Monday \nafternoon. Until then, moisture pivoting around the low will \ncontinue to deliver scattered to broken skies over southeastern \nWA and the Idaho Panhandle with periods of light rain and \nmountain snow showers. Would not rule out a few snowflakes over\nthe Palouse overnight. Clearing will arrive in Central WA \ntonight with mostly sunny conditions anticipated through Monday.\nThe gusty north to northeast winds will slowly wane through the\nevening and overnight hours but remain steady enough such that \nconfidence is low that our valley locations still within growing\nseason, cool enough for widespread freezes. Thinking there will\nbe patchy freezing temperatures around Omak, Moses Lake, and \nWenatchee, mainly where there is strong sheltering from the \nwinds and will be holding off on any freeze warnings for Monday \nmorning but did issue a freeze watch for Tuesday morning (more \nbelow). Clouds will insulate the L-C Valley and Pomeroy area \nwith lower concerns. A hard freeze is expected again for our \nmountain northern valleys away from the Purcell Trench. Clouds \nand threat for showers will slip southeast away from the Blue \nMountains, Palouse, and L-C Valley Monday afternoon with \nclearing skies and lighter winds region- wide.\n\nTuesday-Friday: The upcoming week will bring low weather \nimpacts. Weak high pressure over the region flattens some on \nWednesday with a passing wave then amplifies again Thursday and \nFriday. The main impact will be chilly morning lows. The \ncombination of light winds, clear skies, and low dewpoints \nsuggest Tuesday morning will bring increased probabilities for \nfreezing temperatures. NBM probabilities are currently sitting \nat 15% for Omak, 50% for Moses Lake, and less than 10% for \nWenatchee and Chelan. Probabilities are 5-15% across the \nPomeroy area and L-C Valley. For Pomeroy and the L-C Valley, \nthis is likely due to elevated dewpoints but many outlying \ncommunities are forecast to be near 30F. Consequently, freeze \nwatches have been issued for Monday night into Tuesday morning \nand will be watching trends around Wenatchee and Chelan to see \nif this corridor needs to added. \n\nOtherwise, temperatures will slowly moderate through the week.  \nThe wave passing through on Wednesday is trending weaker with \nmainly passing clouds and 20-30% chance for light showers on the\nCascade Crest. This will bring the best chance for afternoon \nbreezes of 15-20 mph, otherwise the weather pattern will feature\nlight, terrain driven winds. \n\nSaturday-Monday: There remains good agreement amongst the \nensemble forecast suites for a breakdown of the upper-level \nridge as a deeper trough swings out of the Gulf of AK. The \nbreakdown looks to come in different pulses with the initial \nblows over the weekend mainly producing gusty winds and little \nin the way of precipitation then increasing precipitation \nchances heading into the new work-week as the trough becomes \nmore established over the Northwest. Still a lot of details to \niron out over the course of the next week but overall, this \nseems to have support from over 70% of the ensembles which is \nleading to at least moderate confidence for a pattern change by \nthis point. /sb",
